3567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2207 0 R
/E (You avoid giving direct feedback because you don't want to hurt people. Or you give feedback so bluntly that people feel attacked rather than helped. You're either too nice or too hard and neither works.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
3568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2207 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
3569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2207 0 R
/E (Kim Scott developed Radical Candor at Google and Apple as a two-axis model of leadership communication. The axes are Care Personally \(do you genuinely care about the person as a human being?\) and Challenge Directly \(do you say what you think clearly and specifically?\). High on both = Radical Candor \(the goal\). High challenge, low care = Obnoxious Aggression. High care, low challenge = Ruinous Empathy \(the most common failure\). Low on both = Manipulative Insincerity. Most founders default to Ruinous Empathy - they avoid the hard conversation because they care about the person, but the kindest thing they could do is have it.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
3570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14387 0 R
/E (Before any difficult feedback, check: is this coming from genuine care, or is it about you?)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
3571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14388 0 R
/E (Challenge directly means saying the specific thing, not softening it to the point of unrecognisability.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
3572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14389 0 R
/E (Start by soliciting candid feedback about yourself before expecting it from others it earns trust.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
