3927 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2218 0 R
/E (Work keeps falling through the cracks at the same points. Two people think someone else owns the handoff. Processes that should be smooth have invisible gaps that only become visible when something goes wrong.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
3928 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2218 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
3929 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2218 0 R
/E (A Swimlane diagram maps a process across multiple roles or departments, with each role occupying its own horizontal lane. Every action in the process is placed in the lane of the person responsible. Handoffs  where work moves from one lane to another, become immediately visible. The diagram reveals where delays, duplications, and accountability gaps cluster. For complex multi-team processes, it is the clearest way to see the whole picture at once. AI can generate swimlane descriptions and identify the highest-risk handoffs when given process context.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
3930 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14500 0 R
/E (Map each role involved in the process as a separate lane. 2. Mark every handoff explicitly,  the arrows between lanes are where most failures happen.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
3931 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14501 0 R
/E (Look for lanes with the most incoming arrows, that person is your bottleneck.)
/Pg 59 0 R
/K [9]
>>
