3995 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2220 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
3996 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2220 0 R
/E (Eliyahu Goldratt introduced the Theory of Constraints in his 1984 novel The Goal. The core insight is that every system has exactly one constraint at any given time the bottleneck that limits overall throughput. Improving anything that is not the constraint does not improve the system. The Five Focusing Steps are: Identify the constraint, Exploit it \(get maximum output from it without major investment\), Subordinate everything else \(optimise the system to support the constraint\), Elevate it \(invest to increase its capacity\), and Repeat \(once solved, find the next constraint\).)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
3997 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14521 0 R
/E (Map your entire delivery process and find where work piles up  that is your constraint.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
3998 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14522 0 R
/E (The constraint is almost never where you think it is - it is where queue builds up, not where people are busiest.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
3999 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14523 0 R
/E (Before investing in fixing the constraint, exploit it first, squeeze maximum output from what you have.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
