4211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2226 0 R
/E (Turn every experiment into a learning that compounds)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
4212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2226 0 R
/E (Your team tries things, some work and some don't, but the learning doesn't carry forward. The same mistakes reappear. Successful experiments don't get systematised. You're improving through trial and error instead of through a repeatable process.)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
4213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2226 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
4214 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2226 0 R
/E (The PDCA \(Plan-Do-Check-Act\) cycle was developed by W. Edwards Deming in the 1950s and became the foundation of continuous improvement philosophy in Japan and beyond. Plan \(define the problem, form a hypothesis, design the experiment\), Do \(run the experiment on a small scale\), Check \(analyse the results against the hypothesis\), Act \(standardise what worked, abandon what didn't, run the next cycle\). The cycle is designed to be continuous  every Act step feeds the next Plan. When PDCA is embedded in a team's operating rhythm, the team improves structurally, not randomly.)
/Pg 67 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
4215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14589 0 R
