4406 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2232 0 R
/E (Your team fixes problems reactively something breaks, you fix it, you move on. There is no systematic habit of looking for small improvements. Progress happens in large infrequent jumps instead of steady daily gains.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
4407 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2232 0 R
/E (Kaizen \(Japanese: kai = change, zen = good\) is the philosophy and practice of continuous, incremental improvement developed as a core principle of the Toyota Production System in postwar Japan. It is not a project it is a culture. The central idea is that every person at every level, every day, is looking for one small thing to improve. No improvement is too small. The compound effect of thousands of small improvements creates transformation that no single large project can match. Masaaki Imai brought Kaizen to Western management through his 1986 book of the same name.)
/Pg 73 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
