4777 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2243 0 R
/E (You are about to launch something, make a major hire, or commit significant resources  and you want to stress-test it before you commit. But your team is optimistic and no one is raising the hard questions.)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
4778 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2243 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
4779 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2243 0 R
/E (The Pre-Mortem was developed by psychologist Gary Klein as an antidote to groupthink and planning optimism. Before a project begins, the team imagines it is one year in the future and the project has failed spectacularly. Each person writes down every reason they can think of for why it failed. The group then reviews all reasons and identifies the most likely and most serious failure modes. Unlike a risk register \(which asks 'what could go wrong?'\), the Pre-Mortem asks 'what did go wrong?'  the past tense activates a different and more honest mode of thinking.)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
4780 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14749 0 R
/E (Run the Pre-Mortem in writing first, verbal Pre-Mortems are dominated by the loudest voice.)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
4781 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14750 0 R
/E (Separate generation from evaluation, collect all failure reasons before discussing any of them.)
/Pg 84 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
