4983 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2249 0 R
/E (Plan for the futures that haven't happened yet, including the ones you're not expecting)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
4984 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2249 0 R
/E (You plan for one future: the one you expect. When the market shifts, a competitor moves, or an external event disrupts your assumptions, you are caught without a contingency. You are not naive, you just haven't built the habit of planning for multiple futures.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
4985 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2249 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
4986 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2249 0 R
/E (Scenario Planning was pioneered by Herman Kahn at the RAND Corporation and developed into a strategic tool by the Shell Oil planning team in the 1970s, most famously used to prepare for the 1973 oil crisis before it happened. The method builds three to four internally consistent, plausible futures based on the key uncertainties in your environment. You then stress-test your strategy against each scenario. The goal is not to predict which future will happen, it is to build a strategy that is robust across multiple futures and to identify the early signals that tell you which future is emerging.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
4987 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14817 0 R
/E (Identify your two most critical and most uncertain variables, these become the axes of your scenario matrix.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
4988 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14818 0 R
/E (Build scenarios that are plausible, distinct, and internally consistent  not best/worst/base.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
4989 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14819 0 R
/E (Name each scenario memorably, it makes them easier to reference in team conversations.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
4990 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 14820 0 R
/E (Define the early warning signals for each scenario before you need them.)
/Pg 90 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
4991 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2249 0 R
/E (THE FRAMEWORK)
/Pg 90 0 R
