5052 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2251 0 R
/E (Your team has reached consensus on a strategy or plan. Everyone is aligned and optimistic. But consensus is not the same as correctness and optimism is not the same as rigor. You need someone to try to break the plan before you execute it.)
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
5053 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2251 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
5054 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2251 0 R
/E (Red Teaming originates from US military and CIA practice,  a dedicated team whose sole job is to find weaknesses in friendly plans by thinking like the adversary. In business, Red Team Thinking means assembling a small group \(or prompting AI\) to argue as hard as possible against your plan, assumption, or strategy. Unlike a Pre-Mortem \(which imagines failure after the fact\), Red Team Thinking operates in real time, it is an adversarial stress test of your current thinking. The goal is not to be negative but to find the genuine weaknesses before they are exploited by reality.)
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
5055 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 2251 0 R
/Pg 92 0 R
/A [14837 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
5056 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2251 0 R
/E (The moment you defend your plan instead of listening, the Red Team session is over, everyone in the room reads it, and the honest attacks stop coming. Rule 2 exists for a reason: a Red Team that gets argued with becomes a Red Team that goes quiet.)
/Pg 92 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
