<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2270 0 R
/E (You have a real business but your fundraising conversations are not landing. Investors say they'll think about it. Your deck is comprehensive but the narrative is not compelling. You're presenting data when you should be telling a story.)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
5625 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2270 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
5626 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2270 0 R
/E (The Pitch Spine is a narrative structure synthesised from the YC application framework, Sequoia's pitch deck template, and the StoryBrand methodology. It has seven beats: The World As It Was \(the status quo that creates the problem\), The Problem \(specific, painful, underserved\), The Insight \(the non-obvious truth that makes your solution possible\), The Solution \(your specific approach  why now, why this\), The Proof \(traction, metrics, or validation\), The Ask \(exactly what you need and why\), and The Vision \(the world your company creates at scale\). Each beat must earn the next  if the investor is not nodding by The Insight, The Solution will not land.)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
5627 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15030 0 R
/E (Write The Insight first, it is the hardest and most important beat. If it is obvious, rewrite it.)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
5628 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15031 0 R
/E (The Problem must be painful enough that the investor immediately wants a solution. If it doesn't hurt, deepen it)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
5629 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15032 0 R
/E (Proof does not have to be revenue, it can be retention, NPS, letters of intent, or waitlist size.)
/Pg 111 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
