<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2272 0 R
/E (You have a single financial plan that assumes everything goes roughly as expected. When reality diverges  and it always does , you have no framework for how to respond. Every deviation triggers a re-plan from scratch.)
/Pg 113 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
5686 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2272 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 113 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
5687 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2272 0 R
/E (The 3-Scenario Financial Model is a planning practice used by CFOs and serious operators to replace single-point forecasting with range forecasting. The three scenarios are: Base Case \(most likely trajectory - what you genuinely expect\), Bull Case \(everything works - best realistic outcome, not fantasy\), and Bear Case \(things go worse than expected - what you plan for but hope doesn't happen\). The model forces you to define the key drivers of each scenario and the decision triggers, what happens in Month 3 that tells you which scenario you're in, and what you do about it.)
/Pg 113 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
