>>
endobj
6067 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2283 0 R
/E (Annual plans lose relevance by February. Weekly plans are too reactive to build toward anything significant. You need a planning rhythm that bridges long-term vision and short-term action  one that is committed enough to drive behaviour but flexible enough to accommodate reality.)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
6068 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2283 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
6069 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2283 0 R
/E (The 90-day planning framework, popularised by Michael Watkins in The First 90 Days and widely adopted in the startup and executive world, uses a 13-week window as the fundamental planning unit. Ninety days is long enough to complete a meaningful initiative, short enough to stay relevant, and naturally aligns with quarterly business rhythms. A well-structured 90-day plan has three elements: three Breakthrough Goals \(the outcomes that will make this quarter meaningful\), Weekly Milestones \(the proof that each goal is on track\), and a Weekly Review habit \(the discipline that closes the loop\). The framework fails most often not from picking the wrong goals but from picking too many. Three genuinely completed goals beat seven half-finished ones, and the discipline of saying no to the fourth goal is where most of the real planning work happens.)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
6070 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15172 0 R
/E (Choose three Breakthrough Goals  not ten. Three that are completed beats ten that are half-done.)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
6071 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15173 0 R
/E (Each goal should be uncomfortable to commit to  if it feels safe, lower the ambition or pick a different goal.)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
6072 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15174 0 R
/E (Build weekly milestones before the quarter starts, if you can't describe progress at week 4, the goal is not specific enough.)
/Pg 124 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
