/E (You end most weeks with a vague sense of what happened and start most weeks reactive  responding to what arrives rather than acting on what matters. Your productivity is high but your progress on the things that matter most is slow.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
6131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2285 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
6132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2285 0 R
/E (David Allen's Getting Things Done \(GTD\) system includes a Weekly Review as its highest-leverage practice. The 5-15 Report, popularised in the management literature - is a compact weekly review format: 5 minutes reviewing what you accomplished, 10 minutes planning what matters next week. Combined with GTD's capture-and-process discipline, the weekly review is the habit that keeps your system honest and your priorities clear. AI can run the review with you when given the right inputs, turning 15 minutes of structured reflection into a clean weekly action list.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
6133 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15189 0 R
/E (Schedule the weekly review at the same time every week - Friday afternoon or Sunday morning work well for most founders.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
6134 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15190 0 R
/E (Do not start next week's planning until you have finished this week's review - close the loop before opening the next one.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
6135 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15191 0 R
/E (The review is not a to-do list audit - it is a priorities audit. Ask whether what you worked on this week actually mattered.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
6136 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15192 0 R
/E (Keep the review under 20 minutes. If it takes longer, the system needs simplification, not more time.)
/Pg 126 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
