6318 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2291 0 R
/E (You know what habits would make you a better founder. You have tried to build them before and failed. The problem is not motivation  it is system design. You are relying on willpower rather than building an environment that makes the right behaviour automatic.)
/Pg 132 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
6319 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2291 0 R
/E (HOW TO USE IT)
/Pg 132 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
6320 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2291 0 R
/E (James Clear's Atomic Habits \(2018\) synthesises decades of behaviour science into four laws of behaviour change: Make it Obvious \(cue\), Make it Attractive \(craving\), Make it Easy \(response\), Make it Satisfying \(reward\). Habit Stacking, attaching a new habit to an existing one, is the highest-leverage application of these laws.)
/Pg 132 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
6321 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2291 0 R
/E (The formula is: After I [CURRENT HABIT], I will [NEW HABIT]. This eliminates the need for a separate trigger and uses the existing neural pathway as the bridge. The habit succeeds or fails on how tightly it rides that existing routine, not on how much willpower you bring to it. AI can design a complete habit stack for any goal when given your current daily routine.)
/Pg 132 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
6322 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15248 0 R
/E (Map your existing daily habits before designing new ones - stacking requires anchors.)
/Pg 132 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
6323 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15249 0 R
/E (Start with the smallest possible version of each habit - two minutes maximum. Reduce friction to zero.)
/Pg 132 0 R
/K [12]
