6591 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2299 0 R
/E (Hansei \(Japanese: han = to change, sei = to look back\) is a reflective practice embedded in Japanese culture and the Toyota Production System. It is the discipline of honest self-examination,  acknowledging mistakes not with shame, but with genuine curiosity and the commitment to improve. In Toyota, Hansei is practiced after every significant project or event, not as a performance review but as a personal reflection ritual. Unlike Western post-mortems \(which are group analyses\), Hansei is individual, it asks the leader to look inward honestly before examining external factors. It has three movements: acknowledge what happened honestly, take personal responsibility for your role in it, and commit to a specific change.)
/Pg 140 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
6592 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 2299 0 R
/E (You move from project to project, quarter to quarter, without pausing to genuinely examine what happened and what it means. You learn from experience, but not as systematically or as honestly as you could. Growth happens, but not by design.)
/Pg 140 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
6593 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15343 0 R
/E (Practice Hansei weekly,  not only after failures. Examining successes with the same honesty is equally valuable.)
/Pg 140 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
6594 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15344 0 R
/E (The first question in Hansei is always personal: 'What was my role in this outcome?' before 'What caused this externally?')
/Pg 140 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
6595 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 15345 0 R
/E (Hansei without a specific commitment to change is just reflection. The practice ends with a behaviour change, not a conclusion.)
/Pg 140 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
